# 11. Forms, Interaction & Feedback

## 11.1 Form Lifecycle

```text
Idle
 ↓
Editing
 ↓
Validating
 ↓
Submitting
 ↓
Success
```

Failure returns to a recoverable state.

## 11.2 Validation

Client validation:

- immediate feedback
- formatting
- basic constraints

Backend validation:

- authoritative business rules
- permissions
- consistency

## 11.3 Mutation States

Every mutation must communicate:

- idle
- submitting
- success
- failure
- retry where safe

## 11.4 Destructive Actions

Use confirmation when appropriate.

## 11.5 Modal

Use for focused short actions.

Examples:

- Create Task
- Edit Task
- Add Risk
- Confirm Delete
- Assign Student

## 11.6 Drawer

Use for contextual secondary information.

## 11.7 Toast

Use for lightweight feedback.

Do not put essential information only in a transient toast.

## 11.8 Alerts

Use for persistent or important context.

## 11.9 Empty State

Must explain:

1. what is empty
2. why it may be empty
3. what the user can do next

## 11.10 Error State

Must explain:

- what failed
- whether data was affected
- whether retry is possible
- next action

## 11.11 Loading

Prefer:

- skeletons
- partial loading
- inline progress

over unnecessary full-page spinners.

## 11.12 Optimistic UI

Optimistic updates are allowed only when:

- the operation is safe to predict
- rollback is defined
- canonical reconciliation follows

Otherwise use confirmed mutations.

